#: ../../source/qs_README.md:41
msgid ""
"**qs** allows you to create nested objects within your query strings, by "
"surrounding the name of sub-keys with square brackets `[]`. For example, "
"the string `'foo[bar]=baz'` converts to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:52
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"When using the `plainObjects` option the parsed value is returned as a "
"null object, created via `{ __proto__: null }` and as such you should be "
"aware that prototype methods will not exist on it and a user may set "
"those names to whatever value they like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:59
msgid ""
"By default parameters that would overwrite properties on the object "
"prototype are ignored, if you wish to keep the data from those fields "
"either use `plainObjects` as mentioned above, or set `allowPrototypes` to"
" `true` which will allow user input to overwrite those properties. "
"*WARNING* It is generally a bad idea to enable this option as it can "
"cause problems when attempting to use the properties that have been "
"overwritten. Always be careful with this option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:68
msgid "URI encoded strings work too:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:76
msgid "You can also nest your objects, like `'foo[bar][baz]=foobarbaz'`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:88
msgid ""
"By default, when nesting objects **qs** will only parse up to 5 children "
"deep. This means if you attempt to parse a string like "
"`'a[b][c][d][e][f][g][h][i]=j'` your resulting object will be:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:111
msgid ""
"This depth can be overridden by passing a `depth` option to "
"`qs.parse(string, [options])`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:118
msgid ""
"You can configure **qs** to throw an error when parsing nested input "
"beyond this depth using the `strictDepth` option (defaulted to false):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:129
msgid ""
"The depth limit helps mitigate abuse when **qs** is used to parse user "
"input, and it is recommended to keep it a reasonably small number. The "
"strictDepth option adds a layer of protection by throwing an error when "
"the limit is exceeded, allowing you to catch and handle such cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:131
msgid ""
"For similar reasons, by default **qs** will only parse up to 1000 "
"parameters. This can be overridden by passing a `parameterLimit` option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:138
msgid ""
"If you want an error to be thrown whenever the a limit is exceeded (eg, "
"`parameterLimit`, `arrayLimit`), set the `throwOnLimitExceeded` option to"
" `true`. This option will generate a descriptive error if the query "
"string exceeds a configured limit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:148
msgid ""
"When `throwOnLimitExceeded` is set to `false` (default), **qs** will "
"parse up to the specified `parameterLimit` and ignore the rest without "
"throwing an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:150
msgid "To bypass the leading question mark, use `ignoreQueryPrefix`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:157
msgid "An optional delimiter can also be passed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:164
msgid "Delimiters can be a regular expression too:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:171
msgid "Option `allowDots` can be used to enable dot notation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:178
msgid ""
"Option `decodeDotInKeys` can be used to decode dots in keys Note: it "
"implies `allowDots`, so `parse` will error if you set `decodeDotInKeys` "
"to `true`, and `allowDots` to `false`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:186
msgid ""
"Option `allowEmptyArrays` can be used to allowing empty array values in "
"object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:192
msgid ""
"Option `duplicates` can be used to change the behavior when duplicate "
"keys are encountered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:200
msgid ""
"If you have to deal with legacy browsers or services, there's also "
"support for decoding percent-encoded octets as iso-8859-1:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:207
msgid ""
"Some services add an initial `utf8=✓` value to forms so that old Internet"
" Explorer versions are more likely to submit the form as utf-8. "
"Additionally, the server can check the value against wrong encodings of "
"the checkmark character and detect that a query string or `application/x"
"-www-form-urlencoded` body was *not* sent as utf-8, eg. if the form had "
"an `accept-charset` parameter or the containing page had a different "
"character set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:210
msgid ""
"**qs** supports this mechanism via the `charsetSentinel` option. If "
"specified, the `utf8` parameter will be omitted from the returned object."
" It will be used to switch to `iso-8859-1`/`utf-8` mode depending on how "
"the checkmark is encoded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:214
msgid ""
"**Important**: When you specify both the `charset` option and the "
"`charsetSentinel` option, the `charset` will be overridden when the "
"request contains a `utf8` parameter from which the actual charset can be "
"deduced. In that sense the `charset` will behave as the default charset "
"rather than the authoritative charset."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:232
msgid ""
"If you want to decode the `&#...;` syntax to the actual character, you "
"can specify the `interpretNumericEntities` option as well:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:242
msgid ""
"It also works when the charset has been detected in `charsetSentinel` "
"mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:244
msgid "Parsing Arrays"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:246
msgid "**qs** can also parse arrays using a similar `[]` notation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:253
msgid "You may specify an index as well:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:260
msgid ""
"Note that the only difference between an index in an array and a key in "
"an object is that the value between the brackets must be a number to "
"create an array. When creating arrays with specific indices, **qs** will "
"compact a sparse array to only the existing values preserving their "
"order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:268
msgid "You may also use `allowSparse` option to parse sparse arrays:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:275
msgid "Note that an empty string is also a value, and will be preserved:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:285
msgid ""
"**qs** will also limit arrays to a maximum of `20` elements. Any array "
"members with an index of `20` or greater will instead be converted to an "
"object with the index as the key. This is needed to handle cases when "
"someone sent, for example, `a[999999999]` and it will take significant "
"time to iterate over this huge array."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:294
msgid "This limit can be overridden by passing an `arrayLimit` option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:301
msgid ""
"If you want to throw an error whenever the array limit is exceeded, set "
"the `throwOnLimitExceeded` option to `true`. This option will generate a "
"descriptive error if the query string exceeds a configured limit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:311
msgid ""
"When `throwOnLimitExceeded` is set to `false` (default), **qs** will "
"parse up to the specified `arrayLimit` and if the limit is exceeded, the "
"array will instead be converted to an object with the index as the key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:313
msgid ""
"To prevent array syntax (`a[]`, `a[0]`) from being parsed as arrays, set "
"`parseArrays` to `false`. Note that duplicate keys (e.g. `a=b&a=c`) may "
"still produce arrays when `duplicates` is `'combine'` (the default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:321
msgid "If you mix notations, **qs** will merge the two items into an object:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:328
msgid "You can also create arrays of objects:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:335
msgid "Some people use comma to join array, **qs** can parse it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:340
#, python-brace-format
msgid "(_this cannot convert nested objects, such as `a={b:1},{c:d}`_)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:342
msgid "Parsing primitive/scalar values (numbers, booleans, null, etc)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:344
msgid ""
"By default, all values are parsed as strings. This behavior will not "
"change and is explained in [issue "
"#91](https://github.com/ljharb/qs/issues/91)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/qs_README.md:352
msgid ""
"If you wish to auto-convert values which look like numbers, booleans, and"
" other values into their primitive counterparts, you can use the [query-"
"types Express JS middleware](https://github.com/xpepermint/query-types) "
"which will auto-convert all request query parameters."
msgstr ""

